// What each simulation shows, and what the learner can change.
//
// Written for the companion handbook: its diagrams should picture the same
// thing the simulation does, so this says what is on screen, what moves, and
// which numbers matter. `npm run lesson:export` puts it in the lesson export.
//
// Plain data with no JSX, so the export script can read it in node — and kept
// beside the simulations so it is edited when they are. A Navigation lesson
// whose simulation has no entry here fails the test suite.

export const descriptions = {
  'clue-map': {
    shows: `A park seen from above, 900 × 520. Two footpaths, a pond at the upper left, a cluster of woods at the right, a north arrow in the top corner and a scale bar reading 100 steps. Two reference points are marked and labelled: a water tower as an open ink circle at the upper right, and an old oak as a green disc near the centre.`,
    changes: `Five clue buttons, each toggling independently. Two of them — "I'm walking north" and "I can see the water tower" — draw nothing at all, which is the point. The other three each draw a shape: a solid line running due south from the tower, a dashed circle 300 steps around the tower, and a dotted circle 200 steps around the oak. A status line above the map names how many places she could be.`,
    numbers: `The tower sits 160 steps east and 180 steps north of the oak. The three shapes are chosen so the line meets the tower's circle at exactly one point, the two circles cross at two, and all three agree at a single spot 300 steps due south of the tower — which is also exactly 200 from the oak.`,
    handbook: `A diagram should show the same three shapes over the same two reference points, and the single crossing. The two useless clues are worth drawing too — as a map with nothing on it.`
  },

  'zoom-out': {
    shows: `A side-on slice of the ground, 900 px wide, with a dashed straight line across it labelled "a perfectly flat line" and the ground drawn beneath. A marker reads "You" at the centre. When the drop is big enough to see, a vertical bar at the right edge measures it. A caption always reads "Drawn to true scale — nothing exaggerated".`,
    changes: `Seven zoom stops: a backyard (100 feet), a neighborhood (1 mile), a county (25 miles), a state (about 280 miles), half a country (1,000 miles), coast to coast (about 2,800 miles), and the whole Earth. The view eases between them. The last stop cross-fades to a globe showing the axis, both poles, the Equator, the two hemispheres, an arrow reading "turns toward the east, once a day", and a marker at about 40° N.`,
    numbers: `Earth's radius is taken as 3,959 miles, and the drop across a span is R − √(R² − (span/2)²). That gives less than a thousandth of an inch across a backyard, about 2 inches across a mile, about 104 feet across a county, about 2.5 miles across a state, about 32 miles across a thousand, and about 256 miles coast to coast.`,
    handbook: `The curvature must not be exaggerated — that is the whole argument of the lesson. On a 900 px width the bend is under one pixel at county size and about eight pixels at state size. A handbook diagram that makes the bend obvious at small scales would illustrate the opposite of what the lesson says.`
  },

  'globe-grid': {
    shows: `A globe about 240 px across, drawn as an orthographic projection and tilted 20° so the grid reads as a ball. Lines of latitude every 15° and lines of longitude every 15°; the Equator and the prime meridian are drawn heavier than the rest. A marker with a ring around it sits at the chosen coordinate, and the globe turns so that coordinate always faces the reader. Three readouts across the top: the coordinate in degrees, minutes and seconds; one degree of latitude here; one degree of longitude here. A caption under the globe states that the lines of longitude are furthest apart at the Equator and meet at both poles.`,
    changes: `Two sliders, latitude from 90° S to 90° N and longitude from 180° W to 180° E, both in half-degree steps. Five preset buttons jump to a place and print a sentence about it: the Equator at the prime meridian, Greenwich, Columbus (Ohio), Quito, and the North Pole. The latitude slider is the one that matters — the longitude readout falls as it climbs.`,
    numbers: `One degree of latitude is 69.1 miles and never changes. One degree of longitude is 69.2 × cos(latitude) miles: 69.2 at the Equator, 48.9 at 45°, 34.6 at 60° — exactly half — 17.9 at 75°, 1.2 at 89°, and 0.0 at the pole. A second readout line says how many per cent narrower than the Equator the current figure is. The coordinate is shown as degrees, minutes and seconds to a tenth of a second.`,
    handbook: `A diagram should put the two figures side by side at two or three latitudes, because the whole lesson is that one of them moves and the other does not. Drawing the parallels evenly spaced and the meridians closing in on the poles is the point; any diagram that spaces the meridians evenly illustrates the opposite of the lesson.`
  },

  'map-maker': {
    shows: `Two tabs. **Flatten the world** draws Greenland and Africa as latitude/longitude outlines, with a grid every 15°, through one of three projections — a globe, Mercator, or an equal-area cylindrical map. Each projection is fitted to the frame with a single scale for both axes, so the drawn areas stay comparable. The readout says how many times the size of Greenland Africa looks on the picture currently drawn, and then gives the true figure. **Read the ground** draws a topographic sheet 900 × 560, showing a round hill on the left, a longer ridge on the right, a saddle between them, a summit marker with its height, labelled index contours, a ring marking the point being read, a north arrow, a scale bar, and, when switched on, a five-entry legend beneath the sheet and a cross-section drawn under it along the dashed line A–B.`,
    changes: `On the first tab, three projection buttons; each prints a sentence about what that projection keeps and what it pays. On the second tab: the contour interval (20, 40 or 100 feet), a toggle for the side view, a toggle for the legend, and four buttons choosing where on the map to read the ground — the summit, the steep face, the saddle, the broad ridge top. The three readouts give the elevation there, the slope there in feet per hundred and in degrees, and how far apart the contour lines sit there as a result — except at the summit, where the ground is level and the lines ring the point rather than crossing it, so no spacing is printed.`,
    numbers: `Areas are measured by shoelace from the polygons actually drawn, so the readout always describes the picture. Greenland is 836,330 sq mi and Africa 11,724,000, a ratio of 14.0. The globe measures 15.3 at this viewing angle, Mercator 1.0, the equal-area map 14.0. Mercator's area stretch is 1/cos²(latitude): twice at 45°, four times at 60°, 8.5 at 70°, 33 at 80°. On the topographic sheet one pixel is 6 feet of ground, so it covers 1.02 × 0.64 miles. The summit is 1,689 ft, the saddle 1,295 ft, the lowest ground 1,200 ft. A 40-ft interval draws 12 lines, 20 ft draws 24, 100 ft draws 4. The steep face falls 47 ft per 100 (25°) and its 40-ft lines are 85 ft apart; the ridge top falls 9 ft per 100 (5°) and its lines are 433 ft apart. The A–B section is 0.92 miles: up to 1,676 ft, down to 1,286, up to 1,504, then away.`,
    handbook: `The projection figures are the argument, so a diagram should show the same three pictures of the same two landmasses with the measured ratio against each. For the contours, a handbook diagram should pair the map with the cross-section of the same line, because that pairing is what turns the lines back into ground. The contours come from marching squares over one height function and the cross-section is sampled from that same function, so the two drawings can be trusted to agree with each other. There is deliberately no stream: on ground this smooth the line water would take is a straight diagonal, which looks like a drawing mistake rather than a river.`
  },

  'locate': {
    shows: `A map of six minutes of latitude by ten of longitude in central Ohio — 40° 10′ to 40° 16′ N, 83° 00′ to 83° 10′ W — drawn to true scale at 900 × 706. A graticule at whole minutes, labelled along the top and up the left. Six named places with their own symbols: Beaver Pond, Hollow Rock with a spot elevation of 1,284 ft, a stone bridge where Mill Road crosses the stream, a trail junction, a fire tower, and Camp Heartwood. A stream running out of the pond and off the bottom of the sheet, two roads, three dashed trails, woods, a north arrow and a one-mile scale bar. A crosshair with crossed guide lines marks the learner's position. Two readouts: the crosshair's own coordinate in degrees, minutes and seconds, and its distance and bearing from Camp Heartwood.`,
    changes: `Latitude and longitude sliders, in five-second steps, move the crosshair. Five task buttons: two ask the learner to plot a coordinate that is given, three ask them to put the crosshair on a named place and read its coordinate off. A CHECK MY ANSWER button judges it. A correct answer rings the place in green and names it, with how many yards out the learner was; a wrong one gives the distance and the compass direction towards the right spot, and invites another try.`,
    numbers: `At this latitude a minute of latitude is 1.151 miles and a minute of longitude 0.880 — 76 per cent as much — so the grid cells come out visibly taller than they are wide, and the caption says so. Both work out to 102.2 pixels per mile, which is what keeps distance the same in every direction. The sheet covers 6.91 by 8.80 miles. From Camp Heartwood: Hollow Rock 2.17 miles on 037°, the stone bridge 3.84 on 283°, the trail junction 4.35 on 037°, Beaver Pond 4.82 on 327°, the fire tower 5.20 on 355°. Camp, Hollow Rock and the trail junction lie on one straight line, which is why two of those bearings match. A slider step is 169 yards of latitude; an answer counts as a fix inside 0.15 miles, or 264 yards.`,
    handbook: `A diagram should keep the cells taller than wide — squaring them up would undo the one thing the map proves that a sentence can only claim. Worth drawing alongside: the same place labelled all three ways the lesson asks for, as a coordinate, as a bearing and distance from camp, and as a description in landmarks.`
  },
  'magnet-field': {
    shows: `Two tabs, 760 × 470. **A magnet on the table** draws a bar magnet 128 px long with its field as nine traced lines, and a small compass — a 26 px rose with a red north end — sitting at one of four spots around it, turned to whatever the field is doing there. **The Earth itself** draws the globe with a bar magnet inside it, tilted from the dashed spin axis, eleven field lines arcing from one end round to the other, and a compass lying flat on the ground at a chosen magnetic latitude, tipping with the field. Readouts give the needle's angle and the relative strength on the first tab, and the dip angle and the usable fraction of the pull on the second.`,
    changes: `First tab: four places to put the compass (straight above, off to the side, up at the corner, underneath), a toggle for the field lines, and a button that turns the magnet round so every needle flips. Second tab: four places to stand — the magnetic equator, Ohio, Anchorage, the pole — and the same field-lines toggle.`,
    numbers: `The bar magnet is two point poles 128 px apart; the Earth is a single point dipole at the centre, with its axis tilted 9.21° from the axis of rotation (NOAA NCEI, WMM2025 at 2025.0). Everything drawn and every number shown comes from the same field function, so the needle cannot disagree with the lines around it. The dipole gives the textbook relation exactly, checked at six latitudes: tan(dip) = 2 × tan(magnetic latitude) — 0° and 100% of the pull usable at the magnetic equator, 66.5° and 40% at magnetic latitude 49°, 75.1° and 26% at 62°, 89.0° and 2% at 88°. Field strength is quoted in the lesson as a range, 25 to 65 µT, not as a local figure. The geomagnetic pole is 80.85° N, 72.76° W and the dip pole 85.76° N, 139.30° E — about 894 miles apart.`,
    handbook: `The one thing a diagram must get right is that the needle lies **along** a field line and does not point at the magnet. Drawing the compass at the side position, where the line runs nearly parallel to the magnet rather than towards it, makes that unmistakable. For the Earth, draw the inside magnet with its **south** pole under the Arctic and label it so; and draw the field entering the ground steeply at mid-northern latitudes rather than running level, because the steepness is what the dip readout is about. This model is good for shape and poor for the local angle — the Field Guide's Chapter 9 figures should come from the published model, as Lesson 7's do.`
  },

  'declination': {
    shows: `Two tabs. **The two norths** draws a compass rose 236 px across with a degree scale, a black arrow to true north, a red half-arrow to magnetic north set at the declination, a dashed arc marking the angle between them with its value, and a thick accent ray showing the direction actually being walked. **Where, and when** plots the United States as a plain latitude/longitude graticule, 760 × 420, with the agonic line drawn down it, the ground shaded green to the west and warm to the east, and four places marked with their published declinations.`,
    changes: `First tab: a declination slider from 20° W to 20° E, a compass-reading slider through the full circle, and four town buttons that jump the declination to a real value. Readouts give the reading, the true bearing it converts to, and how many yards off two miles of walking would put you. Second tab: the same four towns, and a slider that lets the declination drift forward up to fifty years at that place's published annual rate.`,
    numbers: `Nothing is modelled. A centred dipole predicts about 2.5° east for Columbus, Ohio; the published figure is 7.2° west — wrong by ten degrees and by its sign — so every value was looked up. NOAA NCEI, World Magnetic Model WMM2025, evaluated 1 January 2026: Seattle +14.97° and −0.124°/yr; Denver +7.56° and −0.080°/yr; Columbus −7.22° and −0.018°/yr; Boston −13.98° and +0.071°/yr. The agonic line runs close to 93° W, from four probes of the same model: 30 N 88.0 W → −2.87, 30 N 91.5 W → −0.62, 40 N 93.0 W → −0.05, 45 N 94.0 W → +0.46. Declination counts positive east, true = magnetic + declination, and an error of A degrees costs 1,760 × tan(A) yards per mile — about 31 yards a mile per degree, so 7.2° is 446 yards over two miles.`,
    handbook: `A diagram should show the two norths as two arrows from one point with the angle between them named and lettered — *7.2° W*, never a bare number, because the letter is the half people lose. Worth drawing twice, once leaning each way, because a reader who has only seen the westerly case tends to assume it is the rule. Print the model and the date beside any value, and say that the reader should look up their own: the Field Guide spec asks for exactly this, and Chapter 9 should not carry a fixed figure as if it were universal.`
  },

  'compass:take': {
    shows: `Looking straight down on a field, 760 × 560, true north up, with five named landmarks around you at their true bearings — all drawn the same distance out, because a bearing carries no distance and the nearer ones would otherwise hide behind the instrument — and a baseplate compass drawn large in your hands at the centre. The compass has every part a real one has: baseplate, direction-of-travel arrow, index line, rotating housing with a degree scale and N/E/S/W, the green orienting arrow and orienting lines, and the red-and-black needle. Three readouts track the two movements and the reading, and the reading stays blank until both movements are done.`,
    changes: `Five landmark buttons choose the target. One slider turns your whole body, swinging the baseplate while the needle stays put; a second turns the housing, swinging the orienting arrow round the needle. A toggle labels every part of the instrument in place.`,
    numbers: `The needle sits at the declination, −7.22° (Columbus, Ohio; NOAA NCEI WMM2025 at 1 January 2026). With *aim* for the baseplate's direction and *dial* for the number at the index line, the orienting arrow points along true bearing (aim − dial), so boxing the needle means dial = aim − declination — which makes the dial read the magnetic bearing, and true = magnetic + declination converts it back. Checked at all five: the red barn is true 042 and must dial 049.2; the lone pine 115 and 122.2; the radio mast 198 and 205.2; the church spire 287 and 294.2; the water tower 335 and 342.2. Both checks are within 2°. One degree of error costs about 31 yards a mile.`,
    handbook: `This is the large labelled compass diagram Chapter 8 asks for, and the taking-a-bearing sequence Chapter 10 asks for, in one picture. Draw it at least twice: aimed but not boxed, and aimed and boxed, because the difference between those two states is the whole lesson and a single correct diagram cannot show it. Keep the needle in the same place in both drawings — it is the one part that does not move when the user does.`
  },

  'compass:follow': {
    shows: `A mile of open ground seen from above, 760 × 560. A dashed line runs from the start marker along the bearing to a ringed marker labelled "where you meant to be"; an accent line shows the route actually walked, in eight legs, with a dot at each leg end; a red line joins the two finishing points when they differ. A small compass face sits in the top corner, set on the bearing. There is a 220-yard scale bar for one leg, and optionally a parked pickup beside the fifth leg.`,
    changes: `A bearing slider, a two-way choice between watching the needle and sighting on a landmark, and a button that parks a truck on the route. The readout gives how many yards off the walk finished and a line on why.`,
    numbers: `Eight legs of 220 yards make one mile. The wobble is fixed rather than random, so the result is reproducible and quotable: 3.5, −2.1, 4.4, −1.2, 5.3, −3.6, 2.8, 4.1 degrees, one per leg. Watching the needle, each wobble is added to the heading already being carried, so they compound; sighting on a landmark, each leg starts again from the bearing and the per-leg error is also smaller (22% of the same figure). The truck swings the needle 19° on the leg beside it. Measured, and identical at every bearing because the whole thing is only rotated: watching the needle 206 yards off, with the truck 492; sighting on a landmark 11 yards, with the truck 84.`,
    handbook: `The two paths should be drawn on one diagram, over the same dashed intended line, because the shapes are the argument: the needle-watcher's track is a smooth curve bending steadily away, and the landmark-walker's is a zig-zag that keeps re-crossing. Same wobbles, same bearing, same distance. Chapter 10's exercise is this walked for real, in four legs, on ground where a road stays in sight.`
  },

  'compass:map': {
    shows: `A map sheet 760 × 560 with a grid, north up, three named landmarks on it, and a marker reading "you are here" at the trail junction. A dashed line swings out from that marker at whatever angle the learner sets; when it lands on the chosen landmark the line and the landmark's ring turn green. A corner shows true north as a black arrow, and optionally magnetic north as a red one leaning 7.2° from it. Three readouts give the compass reading, the true bearing it converts to, and how far off the drawn line currently is.`,
    changes: `Three landmark buttons choose what you sighted on, a slider swings the drawn line, and a toggle shows or hides magnetic north beside true north on the sheet.`,
    numbers: `Bearings computed from the sheet, with the declination −7.22° (Columbus, Ohio; NOAA NCEI WMM2025 at 1 January 2026): Beaver Pond is 014.7° true and reads 021.9° magnetic; the fire tower 052.9° and 060.2°; Hollow Rock 084.0° and 091.2°. True = magnetic + declination, so 60.2 + (−7.2) = 53.0, and the line drawn at 053 true passes through the tower. Drawn at the magnetic number instead it misses by 7.2°, which over two miles of walking is 446 yards. The line counts as on the landmark within 2°.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 11's diagram should show the same line drawn twice from one known point — once at the magnetic number and once at the true one — with the gap between them labelled in yards rather than degrees, because degrees look negligible and yards do not. The line must run off the edge of the sheet rather than stopping at the landmark: a single bearing gives a line, not a point, and the picture should not quietly suggest otherwise. Finding your own position from crossed bearings is resection, and belongs with Chapter 12.`
  },

  'sun-path': {
    shows: `A bearing-and-height plot of the Sun's whole day: a circle whose rim is the horizon and whose centre is straight overhead, with north up and east to the RIGHT — the map's orientation, not a star chart's. The Sun's track for the chosen day is an orange curve across it, with open circles where it meets the rim at sunrise and sunset, dashed rings at 30° and 60° of altitude, and dotted spokes marking due east and due west to measure the rising point against. A sun disc moves along the track as the learner scrubs through the day. Readouts give the sunrise bearing in three digits, how far that is from due east, the noon altitude, and the hours of daylight.`,
    changes: `Four places to stand — Quito on the equator, Columbus at 40° north, Anchorage at 61°, Utqiagvik inside the Arctic circle — three dates (21 June, 20 March, 21 December), and a slider that moves the Sun through seven hours either side of solar noon.`,
    numbers: `Everything is computed from src/lib/sun.js, NOAA's algorithm, checked against published sunrise, noon and sunset times for Columbus and Sydney in src/__tests__/sun.test.js. At Columbus: 21 June rises 058°, noon altitude 73.5°, 15.0 hours of daylight, solar noon 1:34 pm; 20 March rises 089°, 50.1°, 12.1 hours; 21 December rises 120°, 26.6°, 9.3 hours. The rising point therefore swings 62° across the year. At Quito the swing is 067° to 113° and the day is 12.1 hours on all three dates. At Utqiagvik the Sun neither rises nor sets on either solstice, and the readout says so rather than inventing a bearing.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 4's diagram should show the three tracks on ONE set of horizon directions, not three separate pictures, because the thing to see is the rising point sliding along the horizon while the arc rises and falls together with it. Label the sunrise points with bearings, not with "north-east" — the course has taught three-digit bearings since Chapter 10 and this is the first place they can be used on something that is not a compass. Do not write that the Sun rises in the east without the qualification: it is true on two days a year, and the chapter should give the local midsummer and midwinter bearings for the reader's own latitude instead.`
  },

  'shadow-stick:watch': {
    shows: `A stick seen from above as a black dot on open ground, north up and east right, with its shadow drawn as a thick bar swinging round it and the grey curve of where the tip goes all day. Readouts give the shadow's bearing in three digits, its length as a multiple of the stick's height, and the clock time.`,
    changes: `Three places (Columbus, Miami, Anchorage), four dates (20 March, 1 May, 21 June, 21 December) and a slider that moves through five hours either side of solar noon.`,
    numbers: `At Columbus on 21 June the noon shadow is 0.30 times the stick's height; on 21 December it is 2.00 — nearly seven times as long from the same stick. The bearing at solar noon is 360° on every date, exactly, because the Sun crosses the meridian every day of the year. Lengths are 1/tan(altitude), so the trail is cut off below 8° of altitude, where the shadow is already seven stick-heights long.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 5's first figure should be the trail of tip marks WITHOUT any construction lines on it — just the curve and the stick — so the reader sees the shape before being told what to do with it. The caption should say that the shortest mark lies on the true north–south line, and should say "lies along" rather than "points north": south of the Tropic of Cancer the noon shadow points the other way for part of the year, and a reader in the tropics who has been promised north will be exactly 180° wrong.`
  },

  'shadow-stick:methods': {
    shows: `The same ground plan as the watch mode, carrying both of the Field Guide's methods at once from a single shared first mark, drawn as a white ring. A red dashed line runs through the mark made a short wait later and is extended across the panel so the learner can see where it would send them; a green line runs to the mark where the shadow returned to the same length, with the dotted circle that defines it drawn round the stick. Two readouts give each method's error against true east–west.`,
    changes: `Three places, four dates, a slider for how long before noon the first mark goes down (20 to 300 minutes), and a slider for the wait between the two marks (10 to 40 minutes).`,
    numbers: `Errors against true east–west at Columbus, with a 20-minute wait, by when the first mark is made: on 20 March 0.1° at any hour, because the shadow tip runs dead straight that day; on 1 May 0.8° at noon and 14.9° four hours before; on 21 June 1.0° and 21.8°; on 21 December 2.2° and 30.7°. The afternoon is consistently a shade worse than the morning — 23.3° four hours AFTER noon in June against 21.8° four hours before — so a table of these figures has to say which side of noon it means. The equal-shadow method on the same days: 0.09° at the equinox, 0.07° on 1 May, 0.00° at both solstices. The two fail under opposite conditions — the two-mark method is beaten by the curvature of the tip's path, which is nil at the equinoxes and greatest at the solstices, while the equal-shadow method is beaten only by the Sun's declination drifting during the day, which is fastest at the equinoxes and nil at the solstices. Lengthening the wait from 15 to 20 minutes moves the error by about two tenths of a degree: the hour of the day matters, the wait barely does.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 5 asks for the two methods to be taught separately and the first to be labelled approximate. It should go further and say approximate BY HOW MUCH, with a figure and a date — "about 21° wrong at Columbus two hours before noon in December" is a sentence a reader can act on, and "approximate" is not. The chapter's instruction to wait 15 to 20 minutes should carry a note that the wait is not the sensitive quantity; working within an hour of midday is worth ten times more than any wait. Both diagrams should be drawn from the same first mark, as the simulation does, so the comparison is visibly fair.`
  },

  'shadow-stick:compare': {
    shows: `The stick's ground plan with two lines from its base: a green one for the true north the noon shadow gives, and a red one for where a compass needle points. The angle between them is the local magnetic declination, named in the readout with its direction in words.`,
    changes: `Three places, which is the whole point — the shadow line never moves and the needle does.`,
    numbers: `Declinations are Lesson 7's looked-up values, NOAA NCEI World Magnetic Model 2025 evaluated for 2026: Columbus −7.22° (7.2° west), Miami −6.58° (6.6° west), Anchorage +15.52° (15.5° east). Magnetic north is drawn at true bearing equal to the declination, counted positive east, so a west declination puts the needle to the LEFT of the true line. Drawing it with the sign flipped puts the needle on the wrong side of north in every place and looks perfectly plausible, which is why the convention is stated here: true = magnetic + declination.`,
    handbook: `This is the figure for Chapter 4's closing exercise, and it should be drawn for the reader's OWN town rather than a worked example, with the angle left blank to be filled in by measurement. The chapter should say that a result far from the published value means iron nearby or a leaning pin, not a discovery — and that the check is worth doing precisely because it is the only way a reader can verify a published declination without a second compass.`
  },

  'solar-clock': {
    shows: `The equation of time across a whole year as a blue curve — minutes the Sun runs fast or slow — with a dashed marker on the chosen date and the value named on the curve. Readouts give the clock time at which the Sun is actually highest for the chosen town, and then break the gap from twelve o'clock into its separate causes, one line each.`,
    changes: `Four places chosen to make the longitude term obvious — Boston east of its meridian, Columbus west of it, Quito which keeps one offset all year, Anchorage nearly an hour west of its — and a slider through all 365 days.`,
    numbers: `The gap has three independent parts: four minutes per degree from the meridian the zone is named for, the equation of time, and an hour of daylight saving where it is kept. Columbus on 1 November (standard time): +32 for 8.0° west of the 75th meridian, −16 for the orbit, so the Sun is highest at 12:16 pm. Boston the same day: −16 and −16, giving 11:28 am. Columbus on 21 June: +32, +2 and +60, giving 1:34 pm. Anchorage on 21 June: +60, +2 and +60, giving 2:01 pm. The curve's extremes are about −14 minutes in mid-February, +4 in mid-May, −6 in late July and +16 in early November, and it crosses zero exactly four times a year; those are checked against published values in src/__tests__/sun.test.js.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 6 should keep the three causes on three separate lines and never present them as one correction, because a reader who merges them cannot tell which part changes with the date and which does not. The longitude part is the one to teach first: it is constant, it is arithmetic, and it is the reader's own longitude, which Chapter 2 already gave them. The chapter must also carry the brief's instruction plainly — a compass cannot tell the time. The Sun provides the hour and the compass only provides a direction to measure the shadow against.`
  },

  'star-sky:turning': {
    shows: `The night sky from one spot, drawn as a disc: the rim is the horizon, the centre is straight overhead, north up and east right — the same orientation as Week 3's sky and shadow plots. The named navigation stars are drawn in their patterns with the lines joined up, over a fixed scattering of fainter ones, and each named star trails a faint arc showing where it has been over the past six hours.`,
    changes: `Four places to stand (Miami, Columbus, Anchorage, Sydney), four months, a slider across the night from six in the evening to six in the morning, and a toggle for the trails.`,
    numbers: `Positions come from src/lib/stars.js — J2000 catalogue entries precessed to 2026 by the rigorous rotation, checked against published Polaris figures in src/__tests__/stars.test.js. Every trail is an arc of the same angular length, because the sky turns 15° an hour whatever the star; what differs is the radius. The sky runs fast on the Sun by 3 minutes 56 seconds a day, which is one whole extra turn across a year.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 7's opening figure should be a long-exposure star-trail photograph if one can be licensed, because it is this simulation taken with a camera and no reader doubts a photograph. If it must be drawn, draw the arcs concentric and equal in angle but different in radius — the commonest error in textbook versions is drawing them all the same length, which quietly says the stars move at different rates.`
  },

  'star-sky:pointers': {
    shows: `The sky centred on the celestial pole rather than on the horizon, so the night's motion is a plain rotation about the middle of the picture. The Big Dipper and Cassiopeia sit on opposite sides of Polaris with rings every 15° out from the pole, and a dashed line runs from Merak through Dubhe and onward at the length the classic rule prescribes.`,
    changes: `Place, month, and the hour of the night — the last is the one that matters, because dragging it swings both patterns round the pole while the pointer rule goes on working.`,
    numbers: `Merak to Dubhe is 5.37°; Dubhe to Polaris is 28.71°. The rule as usually stated — five gaps on past Dubhe, which is six from Merak — lands 2.62° from Polaris. The best any multiple achieves is 1.92°, at 5.33 gaps past Dubhe, because the three stars are not quite in line. Polaris itself is 37.7 arcminutes from the true pole in 2026.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 7 should state which end to extend from, because "five times the distance between the pointers" is ambiguous and the two readings differ by a whole gap — drawing it as five from Merak stops the line well short of Polaris. The figure should also show Cassiopeia in the same frame rather than on a separate page: the reason to learn it is that it is up when the Dipper is down, and that only reads if both are drawn at once.`
  },

  'star-sky:latitude': {
    shows: `The horizon view again, with a latitude slider in place of the list of towns, and Polaris's own small circle round the true pole drawn at six times its real size — at true scale it would be invisible, which is itself the point. The readout gives Polaris's height and the chosen latitude side by side and names the difference.`,
    changes: `Latitude from 2° to 72° north, the month, and the hour of the night. Moving the latitude slider moves Polaris up and down degree for degree; moving the hour slider makes it wobble.`,
    numbers: `The pole's height above the horizon equals the observer's latitude exactly, by geometry. Polaris is 37.7 arcminutes (0.63°) from the pole in 2026, so its height swings 0.63° either side of the latitude across a night: at Columbus, 39.33° to 40.59° for a true 39.96°. That is 38 nautical miles — 43 statute — of position error from reading the star without noting the hour. Polaris was 44.2 arcminutes out in 2000 and will be nearest the pole, about 27.6 arcminutes, around 2100.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 7 should give the Polaris correction as a usable table rather than as a caution, because a reader who knows the error exists and cannot remove it will simply ignore it. The chapter must also say early and plainly that the method fails entirely south of the equator and badly near it, where the star is too low for refraction to be ignored — a method that covers a third of the Earth should say so in its first paragraph, not its last.`
  },

  'star-sky:south': {
    shows: `The pole-centred view again, set over the southern sky: the Southern Cross and the two bright Centaurus stars, with the Cross's long axis extended by the standard rule as a dashed line, and the true south celestial pole marked by a small open ring with nothing in it.`,
    changes: `Place — Sydney is the one that makes sense — the month, and the hour, which swings the whole arrangement round the empty ring.`,
    numbers: `The long axis, Gacrux to Acrux, is 6.01°. Extending it 4.5 lengths past Acrux lands 2.68° from the south celestial pole; the best any multiple achieves is 2.65°; the Centaurus perpendicular cross-check lands 2.86°. All three are limited by the same thing — the Gacrux–Acrux great circle misses the pole by 2.65° at its closest — so care cannot improve them. For comparison the northern pointers are 2.62° off, which does not matter there because Polaris is waiting at the end of the line.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 7 should quote an accuracy figure for this method, because none of the usual sources do: "about 4.5 times" is repeated everywhere with no number attached to how well it works. The figure should draw the pole as an empty ring, not as a star, and the caption should say that the nearest naked-eye star to it is fifth magnitude and no help from anywhere with streetlights. The Centaurus cross-check belongs in the chapter as a way of removing the reader's own estimating error, not as a way of improving on the 2.65°.`
  },

  'star-sky:meridian': {
    shows: `The horizon view with the observer's meridian drawn down the middle as a dashed line from due north to due south, and a readout counting how many of the named stars cross that line on the NORTH side from the chosen place.`,
    changes: `Place, month and hour. Place is the control that matters: the count changes as you move north and south, and nothing about the stars changes at all.`,
    numbers: `A star crosses the meridian north of the zenith when its declination is greater than the observer's latitude. From Columbus at 39.96° that is thirteen of the stars drawn — the whole Big Dipper and all of Cassiopeia. Vega, declination 38.8°, crosses to the NORTH from Miami at 25.76° (77.0° up) and to the south from Columbus (88.8° up, nearly overhead). A star never sets if its declination is at least 90° minus the latitude: 64.2° at Miami, 50.0° at Columbus, 28.8° at Anchorage. Rising points are fixed for a star and a latitude: Sirius rises on 109° from Miami, 112° from Columbus, 127° from Anchorage.`,
    handbook: `This is the figure for the spec's explicit instruction that the highest point of a star's path does not universally indicate south. The chapter should give the comparison as a rule a reader can apply — the star's declination against their own latitude — and should use Vega as the worked example, because it flips between two American cities and so cannot be dismissed as an exotic case. The chapter should also note that a circumpolar star crosses the meridian twice, once above the pole and once below, so "crosses the meridian" and "is at its highest" are not interchangeable.`
  },

  'chart-fix:fix': {
    shows: `Lesson 10's map sheet, with the ford and the old quarry added behind the observer, and the observer's position now unknown to them. Bearings are plotted back from two chosen landmarks, and where they cross is the fix. A shaded quadrilateral shows every position the fix could occupy given the error set on each bearing, and a dashed line labelled in yards runs from the fix to the position the simulation knows is the true one.`,
    changes: `Which two of the five landmarks to use, and how wrong each bearing is, from minus four to plus four degrees. Choosing the same landmark twice is allowed and gives no fix at all, which is the honest answer.`,
    numbers: `Scale is 380 px to the mile, as Lesson 10 established. The five landmarks lie 983 to 1,140 yards from the observer, deliberately close to equal, so the cut angle is the only thing that changes when the pair changes. With two degrees on each bearing the worst case is: the old quarry and the ford, cut 90°, 52 yards out; the fire tower and Beaver Pond, 87°, 57 yards; the fire tower and Hollow Rock, 68°, 70 yards; Hollow Rock and the ford, 39°, 105 yards; Beaver Pond and Hollow Rock, 25°, 162 yards. The closed form is √(a² + b² + 2ab·cos θ) ÷ sin θ, which agrees with the drawn geometry to 0.1% at the shallow cuts.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 12 should give the cut angle a number and a threshold rather than the usual advice to "choose well-separated objects". The figure wants the shaded uncertainty region, not just the crossed lines: two clean lines crossing at 25° look as decisive on paper as two crossing at 90°, and the whole difference is in a region the lines do not draw. The chapter should also keep the degenerate case — two bearings to the same object, or to two objects in the same direction — because a reader who has met it once will recognise it in the field. One wording to watch in the spec: a third landmark does not improve the fix, it measures how much confidence the fix deserves, and those are different promises.`
  },

  'chart-fix:hat': {
    shows: `Three bearings plotted back from a chosen trio of landmarks, leaving the small triangle navigators call the cocked hat. The triangle is shaded green when it contains the true position and red when it does not, the middle of it is marked as the fix, the gap to the truth is labelled in yards, and a running tally counts how many of the last sets of bearings put the truth inside.`,
    changes: `Which three landmarks — all in front of the observer, or spread round them — how good the bearings are, from half a degree to four, a button for a fresh random set, and a toggle that makes all three bearings wrong by the same amount in the same direction instead of randomly.`,
    numbers: `The truth falls inside the cocked hat one time in four: exactly two of the eight ways three errors can fall, confirmed by sampling at 25.2% for every error size from 0.5° to 4° and for every trio on the sheet. The correlation between the triangle's size and how wrong the fix actually is, is 0.30 — the simulation will produce a 31-yard triangle whose fix is 82 yards out, and a 120-yard triangle whose fix is 45 yards out. A constant two degrees on all three bearings is a different animal: with the landmarks spread round the observer the fix is 11 yards out from a 108-yard triangle, and with all three in front of them it is 81 yards out from a 57-yard one.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 12's section on the cocked hat should lead with the one-in-four figure and the counting argument behind it, which fits in three sentences and is the only version a reader will remember. It must not repeat the folklore that a small triangle means a good fix; the measured correlation is 0.30, and the chapter can say so. The practical instruction that follows is about where to point the compass, not how carefully to read it: three objects spread round the observer survive a mis-set declination almost intact, and three in front of them do not survive it at all.`
  },

  'chart-fix:pace': {
    shows: `A walk across the sheet from the ford to a chosen landmark, with the intended line drawn in green and the walk actually made in red. Two heavy bars at the destination separate the two errors: one along the line, for the distance being mis-measured, and one across it, for the bearing being mis-read. They are at right angles, and the box they bound is where the walker can expect to arrive.`,
    changes: `Which landmark to walk to, how long or short the pace is as a percentage, and how far the bearing is out in degrees.`,
    numbers: `The ford to the old quarry is 1,444 yards on a bearing of 120°. There, five per cent of distance error puts you 72 yards along the line and two degrees of bearing error puts you 50 yards across it, landing 87 yards from the mark. Both errors are proportional to the range, so the comparison holds at any scale: two degrees of bearing is worth about three and a half per cent of distance. The other legs on the sheet are the ford to Beaver Pond, 1,087 yards on 017°, to the old quarry's 1,444, to Hollow Rock's 1,890 on 094° and to the fire tower's 2,091 on 060°. Naismith's rule, 1892, is an hour for every three miles plus an hour for every 2,000 feet of ascent, so six flat miles is two hours and six miles with 1,500 feet of climb is two and three quarters.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 13 should make the perpendicular point explicitly, because it is the reason two bearings beat a bearing and a distance: the two measurements fail in different directions, and only one of them can be taken twice. The figure should draw the error box rather than a single wrong arrival point. The chapter should also be plain that a pace count is a biased measurement, not a noisy one — it gives the same wrong answer every time, so it can be calibrated out, which is the one encouraging thing in the chapter.`
  },

  'chart-fix:reckon': {
    shows: `Three legs run from a known fix with no landmark in sight. The plotted track, drawn from the courses and times intended, runs dashed in blue; the track actually made, with a steering bias and a current added, runs solid in green. The two ends are marked and the gap between them labelled in yards, with the readout splitting that gap into the part the steering caused and the part the current caused.`,
    changes: `Speed over the ground, which sets how long the run takes; a steering bias in degrees; and the strength and direction of the current.`,
    numbers: `A steering bias accumulates with distance run and a current with time elapsed, and separating them is the whole lesson. Five degrees of bias is 154 yards a mile and one degree is 31 yards a mile, whatever the speed — on a straight run. On a course that turns, part of the lean cancels: the simulation's three legs lose 169 yards to a four-degree bias where a single straight run of the same 1.6 miles would lose 197. That 169 yards is the same at every speed offered, while a 0.4 mph current is 188 yards at six miles an hour, 375 at three and 563 at two — it is the hours that multiply it, not the miles. A current is named for the direction it flows towards; a wind for the direction it comes from, and interchanging the two conventions reverses the correction.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 15 should separate the two error sources on its first page, because a reader who thinks of dead reckoning error as one lump cannot act on it. The practical consequence is worth stating plainly: going slower does not reduce dead reckoning error, it increases the part of it the water contributes. The figure wants both tracks on one plot with the gap labelled, and the chapter should give the per-mile figure for a degree of bias, which is 31 yards, because that is the number a reader can use without a calculator. The simulation will also carry the real track off the edge of the sheet if the current is given long enough, and says so rather than drawing the marker somewhere it is not; the chapter should keep that case, because running off the chart is a thing that happens.`
  },

  'chart-fix:running': {
    shows: `One landmark, the fire tower, and two bearings to it taken from either end of a measured run. The first line is transferred forward along the run — shown faint in its original place and solid where it has been moved to — and where it cuts the second bearing is the fix. The readout names the angle the sight line has swung and the worst the fix could be, and says in plain words whether what has been produced is a fix or nothing.`,
    changes: `How far to run between the two bearings, from 200 to 1,300 yards, and how good each bearing is.`,
    numbers: `Running due east from the start, with two degrees on each bearing: 300 yards of run swings the sight line 7° and the fix could be 2,492 yards out, which is further than the tower is away; 600 yards swings it 16°, for 616 yards; 900 yards swings it 26°, for 317; 1,300 yards swings it 37°, for 204. Running straight at the landmark swings the line not at all and yields no fix from any distance. The fix also inherits the error in the run itself, amplified by the cut: with a 900-yard run assumed ten per cent long, the fix is 148 yards out from that alone.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 15's last item is "correcting position with known landmarks", and this is the version of it for when there is only one landmark. It should give the running fix a minimum run rather than describing the method and leaving the reader to judge. The table above is that minimum, and the governing quantity is the cut angle from Chapter 12, which is worth saying so the two chapters are visibly the same geometry. The chapter must also say that running towards or away from the object gives nothing — a reader who has only seen the diagram drawn at a convenient angle will not work that out in a fog.`
  },

  'voyage:clock': {
    shows: `Two tabs. **A clock at sea** draws a chart of the Atlantic from 10° to 70° west with meridians every five degrees, the parallel being sailed drawn across it as a dashed line, the Canaries and Barbados marked on that parallel, the ship's true position as a green disc, the position the plot gives as a red cross, and the longitude the clock error has bought as a shaded band between them. A warning panel appears when the plot still puts the island ahead and the ship has already passed it. **The same sum, with light** draws three ranging stations with a circle of position round each, at twenty metres to the pixel, which do not meet; a button solves for the clock error and draws the corrected circles meeting at a point.`,
    changes: `First tab: the clock's rate from 0 to 30 seconds a day, the days out from 1 to 40, and the latitude of the parallel from 0 to 60 — plus four preset rates, Harrison's H4 at 0.06 s a day, a good chronometer at 3, a house clock at 10 and a pocket watch at 30. Second tab: the receiver's clock error from −1,500 to +1,500 nanoseconds, and a toggle between taking the ranges at their word and solving for the clock.`,
    numbers: `Four minutes of clock error is one degree of longitude at every latitude; one second is a quarter of a nautical mile at the Equator and 0.19 at 40° N. On the parallel of 20°, where a degree of longitude is 56.4 miles, a thirty-day run gives 0.4 miles of error for H4's rate, 21 for a good chronometer, 70 for a house clock and 211 for a pocket watch. Harrison's H4 was 5.1 seconds slow after the 1761–62 Jamaica passage, which this arithmetic makes 1.275 minutes of longitude against the contemporary account's 1.25. Light goes 0.2998 m in a nanosecond, so a microsecond is 300 m of range: on the second tab's geometry the three circles then miss each other by 525 m and a two-circle fix is 508 m out, which is more than the range error because the geometry multiplies it. The solver recovers the planted clock error exactly. Relativity figures are published, not modelled: +45 µs a day gravitational, −7 velocity, net +38, which is 11.4 km a day of range.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 16's diagram should show the time difference and the longitude difference as the same quantity in two units, and it should show the ERROR as a band along the parallel rather than a circle — the one thing a reader must take away is that a clock error has no north-south component at all. The chapter should also give the sign explicitly and say which way is dangerous: a losing clock puts the plot east of the truth, so land appears before the plot expects it. Worth including as a second figure: three circles of position that do not meet, with the caption that the one correction bringing them together is the receiver's own clock error. And the chapter should carry the datum and the geoid, because they are what actually catches a modern reader out — NAD27 against WGS84 is tens to a couple of hundred metres across the lower 48, and an Ohio reference station's geoid sits 34.4 m below the ellipsoid, which is 113 feet between a receiver's height and a map's elevation.`
  },

  'voyage:aim': {
    shows: `A walk across open ground to a footbridge on a stream. The stream runs across the top of the picture, the start point is at the bottom, the intended line to the bridge is dotted and the line actually aimed along is solid, and the cone of possible arrivals is shaded between them. A hundred and sixty walks are drawn as dots along the stream, coloured by whether they landed on the side that was chosen or the other one.`,
    changes: `The deliberate offset from −16° to +16°, how well a course can be held from 1° to 6°, and how far away the stream is from 400 to 2,000 yards. Two presets: aim straight at the bridge, and aim off by the amount that makes the side 99 per cent certain for the current error.`,
    numbers: `Walking 1,200 yards with three degrees of course-holding error: aiming straight gives a mean miss of 50 yards and 104 yards at the ninety-fifth percentile, on a side that is a coin toss, with 208 yards of stream to search if the first guess is wrong. Aiming off seven degrees makes the side certain 99.0 per cent of the time, asks for a mean walk of 148 yards along the bank — and costs eleven yards of extra walking on the leg itself, because the hypotenuse of a long thin triangle is barely longer than its long side. Seven degrees is 2.33 sigma, which is where the normal table puts 99 per cent, and it is the arithmetic behind the rule of thumb "aim off by about two and a half times your error". The technique does not save walking: at every error size the aimed-off walk along the stream is longer than the straight-ahead miss, and the test suite asserts that rather than hoping nobody checks.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 31 should give aiming off a number rather than a recommendation, and the number is the eleven yards — a reader who knows the detour is nearly free will use the technique, and a reader who merely knows it is wise will not. The figure wants the dots, two-coloured, rather than a single arrow: the point is not that the arrivals move but that they all become the same colour. The chapter should state plainly what the technique does not do, which is reduce the distance walked, and name the condition it needs, which is a catching feature that runs well beyond the offset — the commonest failure is aiming off past the end of a wall. The emergency sequence in the chapter should keep its first item: at three degrees and a five per cent pace, every mile walked while unsure adds about 130 yards to the circle you could be in, so stopping is not caution but arithmetic.`
  },

  'voyage:assay': {
    shows: `A graph of position error in nautical miles, from 0 to 200, against days at sea, from 0 to 40, for one ocean passage done with four different kits. The latitude line is drawn flat, because it is a measurement retaken every clear night; the dead-reckoning longitude line climbs steadily; the chronometer's line climbs far more slowly; and the lunar-distance line is flat but high. A vertical marker shows the day being read, and the four curves are named in a legend in the upper left rather than beside the lines — four curves crossing each other and a movable marker cannot all be labelled where they run.`,
    changes: `Which kit: by eye and a log line, a kamal with no clock, a sextant and the Moon, or a sextant and a chronometer. Then the days at sea from 1 to 40 and the latitude of the passage from 0° to 60°, which changes what a degree of longitude is worth in miles.`,
    numbers: `Thirty days at a hundred miles a day on the parallel of 20°, where a degree of longitude is 56.4 miles. By eye: latitude 60 miles, longitude 150. A kamal: latitude 30, longitude 150. A sextant and the Moon: latitude 2, longitude 51. A sextant and a chronometer at 3 seconds a day: latitude 2, longitude 21. The slope is the lesson — a better instrument pulls the flat line down and does nothing to the climbing one, and only a clock changes a gradient: 0.7 miles a day instead of 3. The Moon's price is its slowness: it moves 32.9 arcminutes an hour against the stars, so an arcminute of measured angle is 1.8 minutes of time and 27.3 arcminutes of longitude — a penalty factor of 27, which is why lunar distances only beat the dead reckoning after seventeen days at sea. Half an arcminute gives 13.7 miles, against the quarter-degree of longitude the reference works quote for the method.`,
    handbook: `This is the figure Part VI needs at its front, before any individual tradition, because it is the one thing all seven chapters have in common. The diagram should plot error against time rather than tabulating it, since the argument is entirely in the slopes: one line flat, the others climbing. The accompanying text should say that latitude is an absolute measurement and pre-chronometer longitude an accumulated one, and that the difference is a property of the quantities and not of anybody's skill — which is the fairest possible frame for comparing traditions, and avoids the ranking the handoff warns against. Worth stating explicitly: the obstacle was a clockmaking problem, which is why it was solved by a clockmaker and not by a navigator.`
  },

  'voyage:route': {
    shows: `A plan of a day's circuit from Camp Heartwood — the stone bridge, the ford, the old quarry, Hollow Rock, the fire tower, Beaver Pond and home — at 180 pixels to the mile, with each leg labelled in miles, a true north arrow and a one-mile scale bar. A circle is drawn at every waypoint showing how wide the walker's position could be by then; the circles grow along the route and turn green and small wherever a fix is taken.`,
    changes: `Which waypoints to stop and fix a position at, by tapping any of the six landmarks; how well a course is held, from 1° to 6°; how good the pace count is, from 2 to 15 per cent; and a toggle between errors that wobble independently each leg and errors that lean the same way all day.`,
    numbers: `The route's leg lengths are measured off its waypoint positions rather than asserted beside them, so the picture and the table cannot disagree: 1.05, 0.75, 1.14, 1.04, 1.10, 1.43 and 1.93 miles, 8.44 in all, with 1,000 feet of climb, which Naismith calls 3 hours 19 — of which the climb is exactly half an hour. At three degrees and a five per cent pace the two error sources are almost identical in size, since tan 3° is 0.0524 and five per cent is 0.0500. With no fix all day the final circle is 423 yards for wobbling errors and 1,076 for leaning ones, a factor of 2.5, which is near enough the square root of seven legs. One fix at the stone bridge, the first landmark, takes 18 yards off the end of the day; the same fix at Beaver Pond, the last, takes 172 — nine times the value for identical work. Two fixes are worth no more at the end than the later of the two alone, because only the last fix bounds where the day finishes. A fix on every leg never lets the circle exceed 50 yards.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 30's exercises need a planning sheet, and the sheet needs a fifth column: bearing, distance, climb, time, and how wide your position could be by the end of the leg. About 130 yards per mile at three degrees and five per cent is the figure a reader can use without a calculator. The figure should draw the growing circles rather than the route, because the route is the part everybody already draws. The chapter should give the rule that a fix is worth what comes after it — and should say, with the numbers, that a fix at the first landmark is nearly worthless to where the day ends, since taking one early is the universal instinct. Worth adding beside Naismith: the rule is 1892 and famously optimistic, and the honest correction is one day of your own planned-against-actual times.`
  },

  'voyage:challenge': {
    shows: `The same day's plan, with the walker somewhere between the fire tower and Beaver Pond and four pieces of evidence drawn on it: three bearings as lines radiating from the fire tower, the old quarry and Beaver Pond, and a pace count from the tower as an arc. The true position and the best-fitting answer are both marked, and a magnified window shows the hundred-yard region where the whole argument happens, since that is ten pixels at plan scale.`,
    changes: `Which of the four measurements to throw away — or to trust all four. Five settings in all, and the evidence discarded is greyed rather than removed, so the learner can see what it was saying.`,
    numbers: `The three bearings carry +1.5°, −1.0° and +2.0°, which is Week 2's honest figure; the pace count is twelve per cent long, which is Lesson 23's honest figure for a stride on broken ground, and on a 1,003-yard run that is 120 yards. The five answers, in yards from the truth, with how well each set fits itself in brackets: all four 87 (30), discard the tower bearing 81 (33), discard the pond bearing 86 (32), discard the quarry bearing 123 (1), discard the pace count 58 (21). Discarding the counted measurement is measurably the best of the five, and the set that fits itself to within one yard is the worst — because two lines and a circle will always pass through a point, so a tiny residual there means there was no spare evidence left to object.`,
    handbook: `Exercise F is the most valuable exercise in Chapter 30 and the hardest to write, because its answer is a judgement. The two things it should teach are both measurable and both counter-intuitive. First: a measurement that is biased will disagree with ones that merely scatter, so one counted number against three sighted ones is the signature of a bias, and a bias cannot be averaged away. Second, and this is the chapter's chance to say something a reader will use for the rest of their life: a residual measures how well evidence agrees with itself and says nothing about whether it is right, and a perfect fit from barely enough evidence is the least informative result there is. The worksheet should have a column for "which measurement do you expect to let you down, and why", filled in BEFORE the plotting — the habit of predicting the weak measurement is what actually improves with practice.`
  },

  'field:wind': {
    shows: `Two pictures. **A wind in a valley** draws a plan of three valleys with different axes over shaded hills, with grey arrows along the top for the prevailing wind and a blue arrow in each valley for the wind that actually blows there, each labelled with its own bearing; a wind-flagged tree stands in the chosen valley with its branches swept the way that valley's wind is going. **A wind on a shore** draws a lake to the west and land to the east, with the sun or the stars overhead and heavy arrows for the breeze, which reverses between afternoon and before dawn. A banner along the foot of each picture says which parts are a model and which are not.`,
    changes: `Which valley to stand in; the prevailing wind's direction through the full circle; its speed from 4 to 30 mph. On the shore, a switch between three in the afternoon and four in the morning.`,
    numbers: `The channelling is a first-order model, stated as one on screen: a valley sheds the across-valley component of the wind and keeps the along-valley one, so the speed is multiplied by the cosine of the angle of arrival. A west wind at 12 mph over a valley running 330°–150° is felt as a wind from 330° at 6 mph — 60° of deflection. A mile walked on a bearing that far off finishes 1,760 yards from where it meant to: the two walks and the start make an isosceles triangle and the gap is 2 d sin(θ/2), which at sixty degrees is d itself. The course's familiar 31 yards a mile per degree is the small-angle limit of that formula and must not be used at large angles — d·tan θ gives 3,048 yards here, and the walker has only walked one mile. A wind square to the valley produces almost nothing. The flagged tree points DOWNWIND, so reading it backwards is a 180° error. The lake breeze is not modelled at all: land heats and cools faster than water, so the shore breeze is onshore by day and offshore before dawn, a reversal of exactly 180°, twice a day.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 17's required caution — that wind direction is not a universal compass — is better taught as a mechanism than as a warning, and the mechanism is channelling. The figure wants one prevailing wind and three different local winds on a single picture, because that is the whole argument in one glance. The chapter should give the shore reversal its own short section with the 180° figure in it: a sign that is exactly backwards twelve hours later is more persuasive than any general caution. One arithmetical caution for the chapter itself: the familiar "about 31 yards a mile per degree" is a small-angle rule, and converting a large bearing error with a tangent overstates it badly. Worth stating too that a flagged tree is EXCELLENT evidence about the wind at that spot over many years, and used seriously by foresters and wind engineers — a chapter that only debunks throws away a good instrument.`
  },

  'field:water': {
    shows: `A contour sheet of invented country six miles by four and a half, from 974 feet down to 637, with contours drawn by marching squares, streams drawn as blue dots sized by how much water passes through them, and a dashed red line tracing the walk downstream from a chosen start. The ground that drains to wherever that walk ends is shaded — green if the water leaves the sheet, red if it stops in a hollow. A true north arrow and a one-mile scale bar.`,
    changes: `Which of four places to start from; the contour interval, 20 to 80 feet; and a toggle for the shading that shows what drains to the end of the walk.`,
    numbers: `Contours, streams and walks all come out of one height function, so the picture cannot disagree with the figures, and the edge of the sheet drains off it so that no walk follows the border. The opening walk is 2.5 miles and faces through 135° of the compass — more than a third of the circle, from 000 round to 135 — and then stops, because one hollow in the middle of the sheet has no outlet at all and takes 5,273 acres, 31% of the ground. The longest walk anywhere on the sheet is 3.7 miles. Two starts 220 yards apart on the north-east shoulder do opposite things: one runs 2.6 miles into that hollow and the other runs 3.6 miles and leaves the sheet. The hollow is the only place on the whole sheet where water stops.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 18's safety note is the most important sentence in Part V and should be on the chapter's first page, not at its end: following a stream downstream is not guaranteed to lead to people, roads or safety. The figure that makes it land is the pair of walks starting 220 yards apart and ending in different worlds, because no caution phrased as advice competes with a picture of two dashed lines. A note for anyone drawing their own version of this figure from a grid: let the edge of the sheet drain off it, or water that reaches the border will run along it and the longest stream on the map will turn out to be the paper. The chapter should also give the V rule its own diagram — contours bending upstream where they cross a watercourse, and the blunter nose pointing downhill round a spur — since telling the two apart on a crowded sheet is the actual skill. And it should be positive about what drainage IS for: a confluence is one of the few unmistakable features in rough country, so matching the stream pattern to the sheet gives a position, which a compass alone cannot.`
  },

  'field:living': {
    shows: `Forty tree trunks seen from above, drawn as rings in a grid of ten, with moss marked as a thick green arc on the north side, the south side, both or neither. A tree with moss on one side only carries a tick if that side is north and a cross if it is south; a tree with moss all round or none at all carries no mark, because it has said nothing. A north arrow sits above the plot and a banner along the foot states the premise the survey was drawn from.`,
    changes: `Which habitat to survey — a shaded damp ravine, an open slope, or a dry ridge top, each with its own pair of probabilities — and a button to walk on and survey forty more.`,
    numbers: `Nothing here is a measurement of moss. The two probabilities are the learner's premise, printed on the picture, and they are generous to the folklore: the shaded side really is favoured. What is computed is what follows. In the ravine, at 85% and 80%, the odds give 68% of trunks mossy all round and only 29% saying anything, of which 59% are right; the forty trees the lesson opens on come out 30 all round, none bare, 7 north-only and 3 south-only. On the open slope, at 55% and 25%, the odds give 53% informative and 79% of those right, and the sample gives 24 informative with 19 right. The sign is most informative where the moss is least abundant, because what carries the information is the difference between the two sides.`,
    handbook: `Chapters 19 and 20 share this lesson because plants and animals fail in the same way. The chapter's required caution — that "moss always grows on the north side" is unreliable, and why — is best answered by granting the rule its premise and then counting, because a flat denial loses the argument to the first mossy north-facing tree anybody points at. The table to print is the one above: how many trees say nothing, how many say something, and how many of those are right, for a damp place and a dry one. The chapter should name selection bias explicitly, since examining only the conspicuously mossy trees is exactly what destroys the sign, and it should carry Chapter 20's caution in the same breath: a game trail leads to water, forage or cover, and in valley country it converges on the stream.`
  },

  'field:ice': {
    shows: `A regional map of an ice sheet spreading from two domes, with pale flow lines fanning outward from each and high ground drawn between them as a dashed line. Four sites carry red arrows showing the striation direction at that spot, each labelled with its bearing; the two being compared are drawn heavier. A true north arrow sits in the corner and a banner states which part is a model.`,
    changes: `Which two of the four sites to compare.`,
    numbers: `The radial flow is a model — ice spreads outward from where it is thickest — and the consequence is the lesson. Two sites under the same lobe, sixty miles apart, are scratched 7.9° apart; two sites under different lobes, split by high ground, are scratched 42.8° apart. On the geometry alone, sites 60 miles apart and 400 miles from the dome differ by 8.6°, and at 120 miles apart by 17.1°: the spread is the angle the dome sees them at and nothing else. That Ohio's ice split into a Miami and a Scioto sublobe around the Bellefontaine Outlier is documented, not modelled, and is why the sublobes have names.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 21's caution is that glacial markings show local ice movement, not necessarily north–south, and need regional context. The way to make that stick is to kill the mental picture it fights: a wall of ice advancing from the top of the map. Ice spreads from its own middle like honey on a plate, so a striation is a bearing to a dome that melted ten thousand years ago. The figure wants the fan of flow lines, because the fanning is the mechanism and it is invisible at the outcrop. The chapter should also say what these marks ARE for — they are how the ice sheets were mapped, which means a single reading is an input to that work and cannot be inverted to give north — and it should recommend erratics over striations for anybody who wants an actual direction, since an erratic can be traced to a parent outcrop and so gives a line with a known end.`
  },

  'field:survey': {
    shows: `The same six miles of country drawn two ways. **On the survey grid**, a township of thirty-six square-mile sections with a road on every line, the sections numbered back and forth as a real plat is — 6 to 1 along the top row right to left, 7 to 12 the other way, down to 36 in the south-east corner. **In the Virginia Military District**, the same ground with boundaries that follow a watercourse and each other, on no consistent bearing at all. A true north arrow in the corner, and a caption under each sheet saying what its lines are aligned to.`,
    changes: `Which system you are standing in; how good your compass is, from 1° to 6°; and how far this particular survey drifted from the plan, from 0° to 1.5°.`,
    numbers: `BY DESIGN the only thing pulling a Public Land Survey township off true north is the convergence of the meridians, which at 40° of latitude across six miles is 4.4 minutes of arc — 0.073°, or 2.2 yards in a mile, against a compass's 61.5 at two degrees, which is 27 times better. That is the plan and not the ground: the lines were run with a compass and a chain, Ohio's early surveys wander by a degree or more in places, and the Connecticut Western Reserve is on five-mile townships with its own scheme. A survey half a degree out is worth 17.6 yards a mile, still three and a half times a compass; one a degree and a half out has lost the advantage. A boundary run by metes and bounds sits on average 22° from the nearest cardinal, and nothing says which cardinal it was aiming at, so even that is the best of four guesses.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 22 should open with the one genuinely good piece of news in Part V — where somebody surveyed and got it right, the ground carries a direction more accurate than any instrument the reader owns, free and continuous for hundreds of miles — and must then say in the same breath that this is the plan rather than the ground. The wording to use is "designed to run 000°, 090°, 180° or 270°; on the ground, check one against your compass first". The chapter should also get the section numbering right, since any reader with a plat map will check it: 6 to 1 along the top row right to left, 7 to 12 back the other way, down to 36. The figure is the two sheets side by side. The practical instruction is to find out which system your area is on before you need it, which takes one minute with a satellite view and is worth more than any other single fact in the part. And the chapter must carry the bent-tree caution in its own text: some culturally recognised marker trees exist and are recognised by tribal historic preservation offices, and the broad claim that unusually bent trees were generally made as Indigenous directional markers is NOT established — most bending has ordinary causes. The honest label is "plausible but uncertain", which is neither an endorsement nor a dismissal.`
  },

  'field:height': {
    shows: `A side elevation, drawn to scale in both directions, of a sixty-foot oak and an observer standing some distance from it. The sight line from the observer's eye to the top of the tree is drawn with its vertical angle marked, the horizontal to the tree is dashed beneath it, and the paced base distance is measured off along the ground. Below the scene, a curve of how wrong the answer is plotted against the angle the measurement is taken at, with the forty-five degree point marked and a dot showing where the observer currently is on it.`,
    changes: `How far from the trunk to stand, 25 to 300 feet; how well the observer can sight, 0.5° to 3°; how well they can pace, 2% to 10%. Two presets: walk until the top is at forty-five degrees, and stand well back.`,
    numbers: `Height above eye level is the horizontal distance times the tangent of the vertical angle, and the chapter's own worked example — 30° at 20 feet — gives 11.55 feet above eye and 17.05 feet in all, of which the observer's own eye is 32%. The error in the answer from a degree of sighting error is 2δθ / sin 2θ, which has a minimum at forty-five degrees: 3.5% per degree there, 5.4% at 20° or 70°, 10.2% at 10° or 80°, 20.1% at 5° or 85°, symmetric about 45. On the sixty-foot oak, standing 240 feet back puts the top at 12.8° and the answer is worth ±5.2 feet; walking in to 55 feet, where the top is at 45°, makes it ±3.3. At that spot the tangent is one, so the height above the eye is simply the distance paced. Turned round for distance, a 100-foot tower at 20° is 275 feet away and good to 5.4%, and at 5° is 1,143 feet away and good to 20.1%. On sloping ground the two angles add: 30° up and 8° down at 100 feet is 71.8 feet, where the up-angle alone gives 57.7 — 19.6% short, in the same direction every time.`,
    handbook: `Chapter 14 asks for the 30°-at-20-feet example by name and should keep it, but it should also note what that example demonstrates: at that size the observer's eye height is a third of the answer, which is the strongest possible argument for the chapter's own instruction to add it. The figure the chapter does not yet have is the error curve, and it is the whole value of the lesson: a valley with a flat bottom at forty-five degrees and steep walls at both ends. The instruction that follows is not "sight carefully" but "walk until the top is at about forty-five degrees", and the reward is double, because the tangent of forty-five is one and the arithmetic disappears. Worth saying plainly that the instinct is wrong — standing well back to see the whole tree feels careful and makes the answer half as good again. The chapter should also give the inverted use, since a known height turned into a distance is the navigational payoff, and the ascent figure for Naismith's rule in Chapter 30 can be got no other way without a map.`
  }
}

/**
 * A simulation family serves several lessons through a `mode` prop, and the
 * handbook needs a different description for each — "the compass" is three
 * different pictures. So a mode gets its own entry, keyed `id:mode`, and an
 * id with no modes is looked up on its own.
 */
export const describe = (id, mode) =>
  (mode ? descriptions[`${id}:${mode}`] : null) ?? descriptions[id] ?? null
